      "version": "",
      "Uid": null,
      "IsMRef": true,
      "Title": "CodeSmile.AnyMotion.Core.Noise.NoiseMath",
      "Summary": "<p sourcefile=\"reference/CodeSmile.AnyMotion.Core.Noise.NoiseMath.yml\" sourcestartlinenumber=\"1\">Pure, allocation-free noise and oscillator functions shared by every product. Rig-agnostic: takes\nand returns plain numbers only, so the same functions can drive a humanoid bone, a camera, or a\nmachine part unmodified. See <code>shake-noise-spec.md</code> §2 and §4 for why this lives in Core and\nwhy per-axis 1D noise was chosen over a 3D/vector noise field.</p>\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "ManagedReference",
      "source_relative_path": "reference/CodeSmile.AnyMotion.Core.Noise.Trauma.yml",
      "output": {
        ".html": {
          "relative_path": "reference/CodeSmile.AnyMotion.Core.Noise.Trauma.html"
        }
      },
      "version": "",
      "Uid": null,
      "IsMRef": true,
      "Title": "CodeSmile.AnyMotion.Core.Noise.Trauma",
      "Summary": "<p sourcefile=\"reference/CodeSmile.AnyMotion.Core.Noise.Trauma.yml\" sourcestartlinenumber=\"1\">Squared-decay burst model (Eiserloh's trauma model): a 0..1 value set by an event and decayed back\nto 0 over time, whose square is used as an amplitude multiplier so a burst starts strong and fades\nquickly rather than ramping up. Pure functions over plain floats — the caller owns the state, so\nany driver can add trauma without this type needing to know what a &quot;driver&quot; is.</p>\n"
    },
    {
